<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venta Belgarum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venta Belgarum was the Roman town at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, capital of the civitas of the Belgae in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It grew near a late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> enclosure at Oram's Arbour and became one of the larger towns of the province, with a street grid, a forum and defensive walls. It declined after Roman rule ended, but Winchester later became the chief royal centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venta-belgarum.jpg" alt="Venta Belgarum" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Trimming · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Wall_at_Winchester_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1743997.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name is Celtic: Venta comes from a Common Brittonic word meaning market, and Roman writers called the town Venta Belgarum, the Venta of the Belgae, to tell it apart from other tribal markets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-silurum">Venta Silurum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-icenorum">Venta Icenorum</a>. The settlement was apparently founded around 70 CE, partly on the site of Oram's Arbour, which had been abandoned for some years. The Belgae had probably held several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> nearby, among them St Catherine's Hill and Worthy Down, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> moved them to the new tribal capital once the area was pacified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The River Itchen was diverted and a street grid laid out. In the first years of the province Venta was less important than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calleva-atrebatum">Silchester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a>, but it outgrew both by the later 2nd century. A bank and ditch were dug round it in that century, and stone walls followed early in the 3rd, when the town covered 58 hectares. It had fine townhouses, temples and public buildings; the forum-basilica seems to have held a temple to Jupiter, Juno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>, and another temple honoured the Celtic horse goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epona">Epona</a>. Large cemeteries lay to the north, at Lankhills, and to the east.</p><p>From the mid-4th century building stopped, houses fell into disrepair and the drains failed, and people gathered on the higher, drier ground. Yet the defences were strengthened, and men were buried wearing military-style belts. The historian David Nash Ford identifies the town with Cair Guinntguic, one of the 28 cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the History of the Britons of Nennius. After the Roman withdrawal in 410, town life seems to have ended around 450, though a small administrative centre may have lasted on the site of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> palace. Cemeteries of the 6th and 7th centuries suggest that settlement revived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Venta Belgarum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A people called Belgae is named only from Roman times, however, and their pre-conquest identity is uncertain; Winchester, the later <b>Venta Belgarum</b>, had a late Iron Age enclosure at Oram's Arbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgae-britain">Belgae (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
